<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ribe</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ribe is a town in south-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jutland">Jutland</a>, regarded as the oldest town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>. It began as a trading place in the early eighth century, linked to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frisia">Frisia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> trade, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ansgar">Ansgar</a> built one of the first churches in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a> there around 860. It became a bishopric, and its Romanesque cathedral dates from the twelfth century. It declined after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ribe.jpg" alt="Ribe" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hjart · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RibeVue.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Ribe was a centre of commercial activity in the early 8th century, and the riches of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> may have originated there, with royal influence. Coins may have been struck there in 720, and whichever king dug the Kanhave Canal may have been involved in founding the town. Its trade contacts were mostly with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frisia">Frisia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>.</p><p>Of over 300 sceatas found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, 216 come from in or around Ribe, most of them of the Frisian Wodan type and probably minted there in the early 8th century. In the early 9th century a ditch two metres wide, a demarcation rather than a fortification, was dug round a 12-hectare area, and later in the century it was replaced by a moat six to seven metres wide.</p><p>Archaeology shows an active and impressive market place in the 8th and 9th centuries and again at the end of the 11th, with little evidence between, so the town may have dwindled or even disappeared. About 860 Archbishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ansgar">Ansgar</a> asked King Horik II for the first Scandinavian church to be built in Ribe, then one of the most important trading cities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>. Excavations have found between 2,000 and 3,000 Christian graves of the 9th century, which show a large Christian community living peacefully with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a>. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> and a cathedral are confirmed only from 948, when Leofdag was consecrated the first bishop.</p><p>Construction of Ribe Cathedral began in 1150 on top of an earlier church, most probably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ansgar">Ansgar</a>'s. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-ribe">Treaty of Ribe</a> was proclaimed in 1460. The town lies in a large region of low-lying marshland and has been repeatedly hit by storm floods, the most devastating being the Burchardi flood of 1634, whose marks can still be seen on the cathedral walls.</p><p>The Catholic diocese was dissolved in 1536 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> and succeeded by a Diocese of Ribe within the Protestant Church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>. Hans Tausen, the leader of the Reformation in Denmark, was its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> from 1542 to 1562. Before 1864 Ribe and its surroundings were an exclave of the Kingdom of Denmark, surrounded on three sides by the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig">Schleswig</a>, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-schleswig-war">Second Schleswig War</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-vienna-1864">Treaty of Vienna</a> drew a new boundary south of the town.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Ribe is a town in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jutland">Jutland</a> and is regarded as the oldest town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>. It is the seat of the Diocese of Ribe and, since 2007, part of Esbjerg Municipality. The cathedral school, Ribe Katedralskole, has its roots in the Latin School of Ribe, which dates back to at least 1145. Among the sights are the cathedral, an art museum, the ruins and moat of Riberhus castle, probably built by Eric V of Denmark in the 1200s, and the Wadden Sea Centre.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ribe” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Towns such as <b>Ribe</b>, Roskilde, Lund and later Copenhagen were small by continental standards, and the Hanseatic League dominated much of their trade.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-denmark">Kingdom of Denmark · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Scandinavia before 793 was a land of farms and chieftains, with rich boat graves at Vendel and Valsgärde in Sweden and trading places such as <b>Ribe</b> in Denmark, founded in the early 8th century, and Staraya Ladoga, about 753, on the route to the east.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vikings">Vikings · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
